# Both the alias and the real host name. Git remotes are written
# git@github.com:..., which ssh matches against github.com, so a block naming
# only the alias never applies to a push.
#
# The keepalive is load-bearing, not tuning. Git starts the remote helper and
# reads the ref advertisement BEFORE it runs the pre-push hook (verified with
# GIT_TRACE: receive-pack starts, then the hook runs), so the connection sits
# idle for exactly as long as the hook takes. Let it idle long enough and GitHub
# closes it, and when the hook finally returns git writes into a dead socket and
# exits 141 with no error text and no branch pushed. Traffic every 20s keeps the
# connection from ever being idle.
#
# KEPT DELIBERATELY, reviewed 2026-07-29, when the dotfiles pre-push hook went
# from about six and a half minutes to 21 seconds and this rationale looked
# spent. It is not, because one repo's hook runtime was never the scope of this
# setting: the file is machine-wide, and every repository on the machine picks
# its own pre-push hook through the user-wide dispatcher, so one slow hook
# anywhere reopens the window with no warning. The cost is one packet every 20
# seconds on a connection that would otherwise be silent. Do not re-litigate
# this from a single repository's hook timing.
#
# ServerAliveCountMax does a different job and is NOT what defeats the idle
# close: it is how many unanswered keepalives ssh sends before declaring the
# connection dead, so 20s x 30 is about 600s of tolerated server silence. The
# interval is the protection, this is the patience.
Host github github.com
  HostName github.com
  User git
  ServerAliveInterval 20
  ServerAliveCountMax 30

Host unprovisioned_yoshimo
  HostName 192.168.1.70
  User pi
  Port 22

Host yoshimo
  HostName 192.168.1.70
